# SPRINT REVIEW & RETROSPECTIVE NOTES
## Care to Cure Patient Retention & Follow-up Management System

*(Simulated sprint reviews for portfolio demonstration purposes.)*

---

## Sprint 0 Review

**Demoed:** BRD, FRD, backlog, MoSCoW prioritization, compliance requirements, success metrics.

**Went well:**
- FRD and BRD stayed tightly aligned via the traceability table, making backlog creation straightforward.
- MoSCoW prioritization mapped cleanly onto the patient journey stages (appointment → procedure → follow-up → retention).

**Could improve:**
- BR-xxx numbering was reused for two different concepts (Business Rules in FRD Section 24 vs. Business Requirements in the traceability table) — flagged for cleanup to avoid confusion in later phases.
- Retention measurement window (90 vs. 180 days) still needs a stakeholder decision before KPI calculations can be finalized.

**Action items:**
- Rename FRD Section 24 items to SR-xxx (System Rules) to resolve the ID collision.
- Confirm retention window with "Hospital Management" stakeholder persona before Sprint 3.

---

## Sprint 1 Review (Planned)

**Goal recap:** Patient records and full appointment lifecycle.

**Anticipated risks:**
- Ensuring cancellation/no-show reason codes stay standardized (not free text) so they roll up cleanly into analytics later.
- Reschedule traceability (linking new appointment to original) needs to be modeled correctly in the schema from the start — retrofitting this later would be costly.

---

## Sprint 2 Review (Planned)

**Goal recap:** Consultation, procedure, and follow-up tracking; consolidated patient journey view.

**Anticipated risks:**
- Procedure and follow-up both need clear "non-completion reason" capture — consistent reason categories should be reused across appointments, procedures, and follow-ups where possible to simplify drop-off analysis (Epic 6).

---

## Sprint 3 Review (Planned)

**Goal recap:** Retention KPIs and dashboard.

**Anticipated risks:**
- KPI denominators (e.g., "Applicable Procedure Population" for Procedure Completion Rate) must be explicitly agreed upon — the FRD itself flags this as still open (FR-041).
- Dashboard filters (date, department, doctor, status) need to be validated against actual query performance once real data volume is simulated.

---

## Retrospective Themes (Running)

| Theme | Notes |
|---|---|
| Traceability discipline | Keeping every story, KPI, and dashboard element traceable back to an FR paid off early — recommend maintaining this through backend/frontend build. |
| Ambiguous business rules | Several open questions (retention window, KPI denominators) were identified early via the FRD's own "must be agreed with stakeholders" notes — good practice to resolve these before backend work starts, not during. |
| Scope discipline | Explicitly listing Won't Have items (billing, insurance, pharmacy) early prevented scope creep discussions later. |